### 3. Defect Type Patterns — Error Handling & Compliance Failures Dominate

Three clear clusters emerge by failure type:

**Error handling failures (3 defects: CARE-PBC-004, CARE-PBC-005, CARE-PBC-010)**
All three involve the system failing to handle an abnormal state gracefully — session expiry shows a blank page, API timeout shows a blank page, and null timestamp renders as Unix epoch. The pattern is consistent: the happy path works, but error states produce either nothing or technically wrong data. This is a systemic gap in negative test coverage — specifically the "what does the system show when something goes wrong?" category.

**Compliance/regulatory failures (3 defects: CARE-PBC-006, CARE-PBC-007, CARE-PBC-002)**
Non-compliant "not covered" wording (CARE-PBC-006), missing ACA disclaimer for 38% of members (CARE-PBC-007), and a factually incorrect "Live data" label for batch-cache plans (CARE-PBC-002) — all three are compliance failures that carry regulatory or legal exposure. Sandra's prior audit concern was validated by the defect data.

**Data integrity failures (2 defects: CARE-PBC-003, CARE-PBC-009)**
Wrong tier copay values during deductible phase and stale plan data after plan-switch are both data integrity defects — the system renders data that is factually incorrect for the member's actual situation.

**Input validation failures (2 defects: CARE-PBC-001, CARE-PBC-008)**
Special character handling and unconstrained input length are standard input boundary failures. These should have been caught at unit or integration test level.

