### 4. Severity Consistency Check — Mis-classifications

**CARE-PBC-007 — Filed as Medium / P3 → Should be Critical / P1**
This is the most significant mis-classification in the batch. The defect affects 38% of the member population (all PPO Select and HMO Classic members) and involves the absence of an ACA-mandated compliance disclaimer. This is not a "nice to have" display issue — it is a regulatory requirement with prior audit history at Carelon. A defect affecting 38% of members and carrying regulatory exposure cannot be Medium. This should be escalated to Critical / P1 immediately.

**CARE-PBC-006 — Filed as High / P2 → Should be Critical / P1**
The incorrect "drug not covered" wording for a drug that is simply not listed in the formulary implies a coverage decision has been made when it has not. This creates legal and clinical liability — a member could stop taking a necessary medication based on incorrect system messaging. The regulatory and member-harm risk is Critical, not High.

**CARE-PBC-010 — Filed as Medium / P3 → Consider Escalating to High / P2**
The Unix epoch timestamp (01 Jan 1970) displayed to CHIP members is not just a cosmetic bug — it directly undermines member trust in the data currency indicator that is an AC requirement. While formulary data accuracy is unaffected, the compliance requirement for an accurate timestamp is breached.

**CARE-PBC-008 — Filed as Low / P4 → Keep**
UI layout overflow with no functional impact on formulary data. Low is appropriate — this is a cosmetic and UX defect only.

